[2026-08-13 20:32:53] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 228008] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N0\nN5 -> N9\nN7 -> N5, N2\nN2 -> N3, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N6\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N5 or N2. Moving to N5 leads to N9 (terminal), so the opponent cannot move and loses — this is a winning move. Moving to N2 leads to N3 or N4; N3 goes to N1 (terminal), and N4 goes to N9 or N0 (both terminal). Either way, the opponent will eventually lose. However, N5 directly leads to terminal, so it ends the game immediately. Thus, moving to N5 is optimal as it wins the game in one move.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N5 or N2. Moving to N5 leads to N9 (terminal), so the opponent cannot move and loses — this is a winning move. Moving to N2 leads to N3 or N4; N3 goes to N1 (terminal), and N4 goes to N9 or N0 (both terminal). Either way, the opponent will eventually lose. However, N5 directly leads to terminal, so it ends the game immediately. Thus, moving to N5 is optimal as it wins the game in one move.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N0, N1\nN7 -> N3, N9, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4\nN0 -> N6\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N0, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N6 → leads to N7 or N8. N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately), so if you go to N6 and then to N8, you win. But N7 leads to N3 or N9. N9 is terminal, so from N7 you can go to N9 and win. Thus, from N6, you can force a win.  \n- N0 → leads to N6, which again leads to N7 or N8. As above, this gives you a winning path.  \n- N1 → leads to terminal (N2), so opponent loses immediately. This is a direct win.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with the opponent having no move. This is the best outcome. So, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N0, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N6 → leads to N7 or N8. N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately), so if you go to N6 and then to N8, you win. But N7 leads to N3 or N9. N9 is terminal, so from N7 you can go to N9 and win. Thus, from N6, you can force a win.  \n- N0 → leads to N6, which again leads to N7 or N8. As above, this gives you a winning path.  \n- N1 → leads to terminal (N2), so opponent loses immediately. This is a direct win.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with the opponent having no move. This is the best outcome. So, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7\nN0 -> N6, N8\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N9\nN9 -> N0, N1\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N9. Moving to N5 leads to N7, which is terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins. Moving to N9 leads to N0 or N1. From N0, the player can go to N6 or N8; N6 and N8 both lead to terminal nodes (N6 → terminal, N8 → N7 → terminal). From N1, the player can go to N3 (terminal) or N0. Thus, from N9, the opponent can force a win by eventually reaching a terminal node. However, if we move to N5, we go directly to N7 (terminal), and the opponent has no move — so we win. Therefore, choosing N5 results in an immediate win.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N9. Moving to N5 leads to N7, which is terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins. Moving to N9 leads to N0 or N1. From N0, the player can go to N6 or N8; N6 and N8 both lead to terminal nodes (N6 → terminal, N8 → N7 → terminal). From N1, the player can go to N3 (terminal) or N0. Thus, from N9, the opponent can force a win by eventually reaching a terminal node. However, if we move to N5, we go directly to N7 (terminal), and the opponent has no move — so we win. Therefore, choosing N5 results in an immediate win.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N7, N6\nN6 -> N4, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> N5, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\n\nLegal moves: N5, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N1.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N2, which is terminal — the next player cannot move, so current player wins.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N7 or N6.  \n  - From N7: can go to N9 (terminal) or N6.  \n  - From N6: can go to N4 (terminal) or N8.  \n  - N8 leads to N4 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N1, the opponent can eventually force a win by going to N6 → N8 → N0 or N4, or N7 → N9.  \nBut from N5 → N2 (terminal), the opponent has no move — so current player wins immediately.  \nThus, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N1.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N2, which is terminal — the next player cannot move, so current player wins.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N7 or N6.  \n  - From N7: can go to N9 (terminal) or N6.  \n  - From N6: can go to N4 (terminal) or N8.  \n  - N8 leads to N4 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N1, the opponent can eventually force a win by going to N6 → N8 → N0 or N4, or N7 → N9.  \nBut from N5 → N2 (terminal), the opponent has no move — so current player wins immediately.  \nThus, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N5\nN2 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N1, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3, N1\nN5 -> N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N5\n\nLegal moves: N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N2 or N5. Moving to N5 leads to N8, which is terminal—so the opponent has no move and wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N9 or N6. From N9, the opponent can go to N3 (terminal) or N1 (terminal), both ending the game. From N6, the opponent can go to N1 or N4. N1 is terminal, but N4 leads to N0 or N5. N5 leads to N8 (terminal), so the game ends. Thus, from N2, the opponent can force a win. However, from N7, moving to N5 immediately ends the game with the opponent having no move—so this is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N2 or N5. Moving to N5 leads to N8, which is terminal—so the opponent has no move and wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N9 or N6. From N9, the opponent can go to N3 (terminal) or N1 (terminal), both ending the game. From N6, the opponent can go to N1 or N4. N1 is terminal, but N4 leads to N0 or N5. N5 leads to N8 (terminal), so the game ends. Thus, from N2, the opponent can force a win. However, from N7, moving to N5 immediately ends the game with the opponent having no move—so this is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N9, N1\nN11 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N0\nN9 -> N8, N11\nN12 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN10 -> N5, N3, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N13\nN13 -> terminal\nN5 -> N12, N3, N6\n\nLegal moves: N5, N3, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N5, N3, N2. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N5 → leads to N12, N3, N6.  \n  - N12 → N4 (terminal) or N2 (leads to N1, N9, N8, N7, N0, N13, etc.).  \n  - N3 → N13 (terminal).  \n  - N6 → N4 (terminal) or N0 (terminal).  \n  All paths from N5 eventually lead to terminal nodes or cycles that end in wins for the player who moves last.  \n- N3 → N13 (terminal). Immediate win for the current player.  \n- N2 → N9, N1.  \n  - N9 → N8 → N7 (terminal) or N11 (terminal).  \n  - N1 → terminal.  \n  Both lead to terminal states.  \n\nN3 leads directly to terminal (N13), so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. This is the best outcome.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N5, N3, N2. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N5 → leads to N12, N3, N6.  \n  - N12 → N4 (terminal) or N2 (leads to N1, N9, N8, N7, N0, N13, etc.).  \n  - N3 → N13 (terminal).  \n  - N6 → N4 (terminal) or N0 (terminal).  \n  All paths from N5 eventually lead to terminal nodes or cycles that end in wins for the player who moves last.  \n- N3 → N13 (terminal). Immediate win for the current player.  \n- N2 → N9, N1.  \n  - N9 → N8 → N7 (terminal) or N11 (terminal).  \n  - N1 → terminal.  \n  Both lead to terminal states.  \n\nN3 leads directly to terminal (N13), so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. This is the best outcome.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N7, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N5\nN8 -> N12, N10\nN13 -> N1, N11\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN11 -> N3, N9\nN10 -> N4, N0\nN5 -> N6, N9\nN2 -> N7, N13\n\nLegal moves: N12, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N12 or N10. Evaluating each:\n\n- N12 leads to N2 or N5:\n  - N2 → N7 (terminal) or N13 → N1 (terminal) or N11 → N3 (terminal) or N9 (terminal)\n  - N5 → N6 → N7 or N3 (both terminal)\n  - All paths from N12 eventually reach terminal nodes; the opponent wins if we move to N12 because the game ends quickly and we lose on our next turn if we have no moves.\n\n- N10 → N4 or N0 (both terminal). This ends the game immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N10 ends the game on our turn, so the opponent has no move and we win.\n\nTherefore, choosing N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N12 or N10. Evaluating each:\n\n- N12 leads to N2 or N5:\n  - N2 → N7 (terminal) or N13 → N1 (terminal) or N11 → N3 (terminal) or N9 (terminal)\n  - N5 → N6 → N7 or N3 (both terminal)\n  - All paths from N12 eventually reach terminal nodes; the opponent wins if we move to N12 because the game ends quickly and we lose on our next turn if we have no moves.\n\n- N10 → N4 or N0 (both terminal). This ends the game immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N10 ends the game on our turn, so the opponent has no move and we win.\n\nTherefore, choosing N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N2\nN7 -> N2, N1, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6\nN2 -> N4, N3\nN4 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N1, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2, N1, or N5. We analyze each:\n\n- N7 → N2: N2 leads to N4 → N9 (terminal) or N3 (terminal). Both are terminal. So N2 ends in a win for the opponent if they play optimally. But since both paths end immediately, the player who moves to N2 wins only if the opponent has no move — but here, after N2, the opponent can move to N4 or N3, both terminal. So the player who moves to N2 wins immediately? No — after N2, the opponent moves to N4 or N3 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and loses. So moving to N2 wins.\n\n- N7 → N1: N1 → N5 or N2. Then opponent can go to N5 → N8 → N6 (terminal), or N2 → N4 → N9 or N3 (terminal). All paths eventually end. But the key is: from N1, the opponent has choices. If opponent goes to N5 → N8 → N6 (terminal), then the player to move after that has no move and loses. So if we go to N1, opponent can choose a path that ends the game on their turn, meaning they win. So N1 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- N7 → N5: N5 → N8 → N6 (terminal). So after N5, opponent moves to N8 → N6 (terminal), then we have no move — we lose. So N5 leads to loss.\n\nThus, best move is N2, which forces opponent into a terminal state immediately after their turn.\n\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2, N1, or N5. We analyze each:\n\n- N7 → N2: N2 leads to N4 → N9 (terminal) or N3 (terminal). Both are terminal. So N2 ends in a win for the opponent if they play optimally. But since both paths end immediately, the player who moves to N2 wins only if the opponent has no move — but here, after N2, the opponent can move to N4 or N3, both terminal. So the player who moves to N2 wins immediately? No — after N2, the opponent moves to N4 or N3 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and loses. So moving to N2 wins.\n\n- N7 → N1: N1 → N5 or N2. Then opponent can go to N5 → N8 → N6 (terminal), or N2 → N4 → N9 or N3 (terminal). All paths eventually end. But the key is: from N1, the opponent has choices. If opponent goes to N5 → N8 → N6 (terminal), then the player to move after that has no move and loses. So if we go to N1, opponent can choose a path that ends the game on their turn, meaning they win. So N1 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- N7 → N5: N5 → N8 → N6 (terminal). So after N5, opponent moves to N8 → N6 (terminal), then we have no move — we lose. So N5 leads to loss.\n\nThus, best move is N2, which forces opponent into a terminal state immediately after their turn.\n\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N6\nN0 -> N6, N9\nN2 -> N4, N3\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N1, N3, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN1 -> N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N3, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N1, N3, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N8 → N1: N1 → N5 → (N5 → N7 or N2). N7 and N2 lead to terminal or further play. N2 → N4 (terminal) or N3. N3 → N6 (terminal). So paths eventually end.  \n- N8 → N3: N3 → N6 (terminal). Immediate loss for the next player. So if we move to N3, opponent wins immediately.  \n- N8 → N0: N0 → N6 (terminal) or N9 (terminal). Both terminal. Opponent has no move. So we win.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 results in an immediate win for the current player. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N1, N3, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N8 → N1: N1 → N5 → (N5 → N7 or N2). N7 and N2 lead to terminal or further play. N2 → N4 (terminal) or N3. N3 → N6 (terminal). So paths eventually end.  \n- N8 → N3: N3 → N6 (terminal). Immediate loss for the next player. So if we move to N3, opponent wins immediately.  \n- N8 → N0: N0 → N6 (terminal) or N9 (terminal). Both terminal. Opponent has no move. So we win.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 results in an immediate win for the current player. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N7\nN3 -> N8, N1\nN2 -> N1\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N2\nN6 -> N3, N5\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N5.  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (terminal) — both are terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N4 or N2.  \n  - N4 leads to N9 or N7; N9 leads to N0 or N2; N0 is terminal, N2 leads to N1 (terminal).  \n  - N7 is terminal.  \n  So from N5, the game can go deep, but eventually ends.  \nHowever, since the game ends when a player cannot move, and all paths eventually reach terminal nodes, the key is to force the opponent into a position where they have no moves.  \nBut every move leads to a terminal node or a node with only terminal paths.  \nActually, all nodes eventually lead to terminal nodes. So the player who moves last wins.  \nWe need to maximize our chance of making the last move.  \nFrom N6:  \n- N3 → N8 or N1 (both terminal) → opponent loses immediately → we win.  \n- N5 → N4 or N2 → both eventually lead to terminal nodes, but the opponent gets to move next.  \nIf we go to N3, we win immediately.  \nThus, choosing N3 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N5.  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (terminal) — both are terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N4 or N2.  \n  - N4 leads to N9 or N7; N9 leads to N0 or N2; N0 is terminal, N2 leads to N1 (terminal).  \n  - N7 is terminal.  \n  So from N5, the game can go deep, but eventually ends.  \nHowever, since the game ends when a player cannot move, and all paths eventually reach terminal nodes, the key is to force the opponent into a position where they have no moves.  \nBut every move leads to a terminal node or a node with only terminal paths.  \nActually, all nodes eventually lead to terminal nodes. So the player who moves last wins.  \nWe need to maximize our chance of making the last move.  \nFrom N6:  \n- N3 → N8 or N1 (both terminal) → opponent loses immediately → we win.  \n- N5 → N4 or N2 → both eventually lead to terminal nodes, but the opponent gets to move next.  \nIf we go to N3, we win immediately.  \nThus, choosing N3 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}]
