not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N4, N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N6\nN5 -> N0, N2\nN8 -> N1, N5\nN3 -> N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3, N8\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N4 or N3→N4. From N4, the game ends (terminal). So moving to N4 wins immediately. The other move from N3 is to N4 only. Thus, the only legal move from N3 is to N4, which leads to terminal. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N4 or N3→N4. From N4, the game ends (terminal). So moving to N4 wins immediately. The other move from N3 is to N4 only. Thus, the only legal move from N3 is to N4, which leads to terminal. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N2, N9\nN1 -> N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4, N1\nN7 -> N0\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N8\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N3, N4, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N3 → N2 or N9; N9 is terminal (win for opponent if reached), N2 → N7 or N8 (both terminal). So N3 leads to opponent winning if they play optimally.  \n- N4 → N1 or N3; N1 → N5 (terminal), N3 → N2 or N9. This creates a chain that eventually leads to terminal nodes.  \n- N1 → N5 (terminal). This is a direct win for the current player if they move to N1, as opponent has no move.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with opponent having no move — a win. N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N3, N4, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N3 → N2 or N9; N9 is terminal (win for opponent if reached), N2 → N7 or N8 (both terminal). So N3 leads to opponent winning if they play optimally.  \n- N4 → N1 or N3; N1 → N5 (terminal), N3 → N2 or N9. This creates a chain that eventually leads to terminal nodes.  \n- N1 → N5 (terminal). This is a direct win for the current player if they move to N1, as opponent has no move.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with opponent having no move — a win. N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N2, N9\nN1 -> N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4, N1\nN7 -> N0\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N8\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N5. From N5, the next move is to terminal, so the current player (who moves from N1 to N5) wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N5 is the only legal and winning move. No other option exists, and all other paths eventually lead to terminal nodes or require the opponent to move first. Therefore, N5 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N5. From N5, the next move is to terminal, so the current player (who moves from N1 to N5) wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N5 is the only legal and winning move. No other option exists, and all other paths eventually lead to terminal nodes or require the opponent to move first. Therefore, N5 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N5\nN7 -> N4, N2, N0\nN4 -> N8, N6\nN6 -> N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N6, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the player can move to N5, N6, or N9. We analyze each move's outcome using backward induction.  \n- N5 → N7 → (N4, N2, N0). N2, N0 lead to terminal (opponent wins). N7 → N4 → N8 or N6 → N3 (terminal). N4 → N8 (terminal), N6 → N3 (terminal). So from N7, all moves lead to terminal. Thus, N7 is a losing position for the player to move. So N5 leads to N7, which is losing for the next player → current player wins.  \n- N6 → N3 (terminal) → current player wins immediately.  \n- N9 → N5 → same as above (leads to N7, losing for opponent).  \n\nAll moves from N1 lead to a win for the current player. However, N6 leads to immediate terminal (N3), so the current player wins in one move. This is optimal. Thus, choosing N6 maximizes immediate win.  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the player can move to N5, N6, or N9. We analyze each move's outcome using backward induction.  \n- N5 → N7 → (N4, N2, N0). N2, N0 lead to terminal (opponent wins). N7 → N4 → N8 or N6 → N3 (terminal). N4 → N8 (terminal), N6 → N3 (terminal). So from N7, all moves lead to terminal. Thus, N7 is a losing position for the player to move. So N5 leads to N7, which is losing for the next player → current player wins.  \n- N6 → N3 (terminal) → current player wins immediately.  \n- N9 → N5 → same as above (leads to N7, losing for opponent).  \n\nAll moves from N1 lead to a win for the current player. However, N6 leads to immediate terminal (N3), so the current player wins in one move. This is optimal. Thus, choosing N6 maximizes immediate win.  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N2\nN8 -> N0\nN7 -> N9, N3\nN5 -> N6\nN1 -> N7, N8\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N1, N3, or N5. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N1 leads to N7 or N8.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N3; N9 is terminal, so if opponent reaches N3, they can go to N8 → N0 (terminal) or N2 → terminal. N3 leads to N8 → N0 or N2 → terminal. So from N7, opponent can win.  \n  - N8 → N0 (terminal), so player who moves to N8 wins immediately.  \n  Thus, from N1, the player can choose N8 and win.  \n- N3 leads to N8 or N2.  \n  - N8 → N0 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n  - N2 → terminal, opponent loses. So choosing N2 wins.  \n  Thus, from N3, player can choose N2 and win.  \n- N5 → N6 (terminal), opponent loses immediately. So moving to N5 wins.  \n\nAll three moves lead to a win, but N5 results in immediate terminal win for the player who moves. Thus, N5 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N1, N3, or N5. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N1 leads to N7 or N8.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N3; N9 is terminal, so if opponent reaches N3, they can go to N8 → N0 (terminal) or N2 → terminal. N3 leads to N8 → N0 or N2 → terminal. So from N7, opponent can win.  \n  - N8 → N0 (terminal), so player who moves to N8 wins immediately.  \n  Thus, from N1, the player can choose N8 and win.  \n- N3 leads to N8 or N2.  \n  - N8 → N0 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n  - N2 → terminal, opponent loses. So choosing N2 wins.  \n  Thus, from N3, player can choose N2 and win.  \n- N5 → N6 (terminal), opponent loses immediately. So moving to N5 wins.  \n\nAll three moves lead to a win, but N5 results in immediate terminal win for the player who moves. Thus, N5 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN