[2026-08-13 20:38:54] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 228008] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N1\nN6 -> N4, N9\nN3 -> N5, N2\nN2 -> N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N8\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5 and N2.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N6, then to N4 (terminal) or N9 (terminal). Both are terminal, so the opponent wins if we go to N6.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins immediately.  \nBut wait: the player who makes the last move wins. So if we move to N2 → N0 (terminal), the opponent has no move and loses. So we win.  \nThus, moving to N2 leads to immediate win.  \nTherefore, the best move is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5 and N2.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N6, then to N4 (terminal) or N9 (terminal). Both are terminal, so the opponent wins if we go to N6.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins immediately.  \nBut wait: the player who makes the last move wins. So if we move to N2 → N0 (terminal), the opponent has no move and loses. So we win.  \nThus, moving to N2 leads to immediate win.  \nTherefore, the best move is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N1\nN6 -> N4, N9\nN3 -> N5, N2\nN2 -> N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N8\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N0. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N0 is the only option and results in a win. No other move is available from N2. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N0. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N0 is the only option and results in a win. No other move is available from N2. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N9, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4\nN7 -> N6, N3\nN0 -> N7, N2\nN1 -> N0, N8\nN5 -> N1, N6\n\nLegal moves: N1, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the player can move to N1 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N9 or N3, both of which are terminal (game ends immediately). So N6 results in an immediate loss for the opponent (i.e., the current player wins). Moving to N1 leads to N0 or N8. From N0, the opponent can go to N7 or N2 (N2 is terminal), or from N8 to N4 (terminal). From N7, the opponent can go to N6 or N3 (both terminal). Thus, from N1, the opponent has options that eventually lead to terminal states. However, the key is that from N5, moving to N6 results in immediate win (opponent has no move). Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal because it forces the opponent into a terminal state immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the player can move to N1 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N9 or N3, both of which are terminal (game ends immediately). So N6 results in an immediate loss for the opponent (i.e., the current player wins). Moving to N1 leads to N0 or N8. From N0, the opponent can go to N7 or N2 (N2 is terminal), or from N8 to N4 (terminal). From N7, the opponent can go to N6 or N3 (both terminal). Thus, from N1, the opponent has options that eventually lead to terminal states. However, the key is that from N5, moving to N6 results in immediate win (opponent has no move). Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal because it forces the opponent into a terminal state immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N9, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4\nN7 -> N6, N3\nN0 -> N7, N2\nN1 -> N0, N8\nN5 -> N1, N6\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the player can move to N9 or N3. Both N9 and N3 lead directly to terminal nodes, so whichever is chosen, the current player wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Therefore, both moves result in an immediate win. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and both options are equally effective, either is valid. Choosing N3 is simpler and directly terminal. Thus, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the player can move to N9 or N3. Both N9 and N3 lead directly to terminal nodes, so whichever is chosen, the current player wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Therefore, both moves result in an immediate win. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and both options are equally effective, either is valid. Choosing N3 is simpler and directly terminal. Thus, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N7, N4\nN9 -> N8, N0\nN6 -> N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5\nN4 -> N6\nN1 -> N9, N3, N4\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N9, N3, or N4. We analyze each option for winning potential.  \n- N3 → N5 → terminal: immediate win for opponent (player loses).  \n- N4 → N6 → N2 → terminal: opponent wins.  \n- N9 → N8 → (N6, N7, N4) or N0 → terminal. From N8, if player goes to N0, opponent wins. If to N6 or N7, the game continues. N7 → terminal, so if opponent goes to N7, they win immediately. N8 → N6 → N2 → terminal: opponent wins. So from N9, all paths eventually lead to terminal or opponent wins.  \nHowever, if from N1 we go to N4 → N6 → N2 → terminal, opponent wins.  \nBut if we go to N3 → N5 → terminal, opponent has no move → we win.  \nThus, moving to N3 leads to immediate terminal for opponent. This is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N3 maximizes our outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N9, N3, or N4. We analyze each option for winning potential.  \n- N3 → N5 → terminal: immediate win for opponent (player loses).  \n- N4 → N6 → N2 → terminal: opponent wins.  \n- N9 → N8 → (N6, N7, N4) or N0 → terminal. From N8, if player goes to N0, opponent wins. If to N6 or N7, the game continues. N7 → terminal, so if opponent goes to N7, they win immediately. N8 → N6 → N2 → terminal: opponent wins. So from N9, all paths eventually lead to terminal or opponent wins.  \nHowever, if from N1 we go to N4 → N6 → N2 → terminal, opponent wins.  \nBut if we go to N3 → N5 → terminal, opponent has no move → we win.  \nThus, moving to N3 leads to immediate terminal for opponent. This is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N3 maximizes our outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N7, N4\nN9 -> N8, N0\nN6 -> N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5\nN4 -> N6\nN1 -> N9, N3, N4\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 results in an immediate win. Any other path would require the opponent to move first from a different node, but from N3, only N5 is available. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 results in an immediate win. Any other path would require the opponent to move first from a different node, but from N3, only N5 is available. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N9, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N8, N6\nN2 -> N1, N7\nN0 -> N5, N2\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N0. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N1 → N9 → (N9 → N8 or N6; both lead to terminal or further moves). N9 → N8 → N4 → terminal. N9 → N6 → terminal. So from N1, the game ends in a win for the player who moves to N1 (since opponent faces terminal after).  \n- N9 → N8 → N4 → terminal. N9 → N6 → terminal. So N9 leads to terminal in one move.  \n- N0 → N5 → terminal or N0 → N2 → N1 → N9 → ... This can loop. N2 → N1 → N9 → N8 → N4 → terminal. So from N0, the game can continue.  \nBut crucially, from N3, if we go to N9, the opponent is at N9, which has moves to N8 and N6, both leading to terminal. So opponent must move to terminal and loses. Thus, N9 is a winning move.  \nSimilarly, N1 leads to N9, which again leads to terminal. N0 leads to N5 (terminal) or N2 → N1 → N9 → terminal. So all paths eventually end.  \nBut the key is: from N3, moving to N9 immediately puts opponent in a position where they must move to terminal (N8 → N4 or N6 → terminal), so they lose. Thus, N9 is the best move.  \n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N0. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N1 → N9 → (N9 → N8 or N6; both lead to terminal or further moves). N9 → N8 → N4 → terminal. N9 → N6 → terminal. So from N1, the game ends in a win for the player who moves to N1 (since opponent faces terminal after).  \n- N9 → N8 → N4 → terminal. N9 → N6 → terminal. So N9 leads to terminal in one move.  \n- N0 → N5 → terminal or N0 → N2 → N1 → N9 → ... This can loop. N2 → N1 → N9 → N8 → N4 → terminal. So from N0, the game can continue.  \nBut crucially, from N3, if we go to N9, the opponent is at N9, which has moves to N8 and N6, both leading to terminal. So opponent must move to terminal and loses. Thus, N9 is a winning move.  \nSimilarly, N1 leads to N9, which again leads to terminal. N0 leads to N5 (terminal) or N2 → N1 → N9 → terminal. So all paths eventually end.  \nBut the key is: from N3, moving to N9 immediately puts opponent in a position where they must move to terminal (N8 → N4 or N6 → terminal), so they lose. Thus, N9 is the best move.  \n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N6\nN8 -> N4, N6\nN9 -> N8, N3\nN4 -> N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N0\nN1 -> N4, N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N4 or N5. Moving to N4 leads to N2, then to N7 or N6, both of which lead to terminal nodes (win for the opponent if they reach terminal). Moving to N5 leads to N9 or N0. N0 is terminal, so that ends the game immediately (current player wins). N9 leads to N8, then to N4 or N6; N6 is terminal. So from N5 → N9 → N8 → N4 → N2 → N7 (terminal). Either path eventually ends. However, if we go N1 → N5 → N0, the game ends immediately, and the current player (first player) wins. This is a winning move. Thus, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N4 or N5. Moving to N4 leads to N2, then to N7 or N6, both of which lead to terminal nodes (win for the opponent if they reach terminal). Moving to N5 leads to N9 or N0. N0 is terminal, so that ends the game immediately (current player wins). N9 leads to N8, then to N4 or N6; N6 is terminal. So from N5 → N9 → N8 → N4 → N2 → N7 (terminal). Either path eventually ends. However, if we go N1 → N5 → N0, the game ends immediately, and the current player (first player) wins. This is a winning move. Thus, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N2, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N5\nN7 -> N6, N8\nN4 -> N3, N0\nN2 -> N4, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9\n\nLegal moves: N7, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N7 or N5. Moving to N5 leads to N2 or N4. From N2, you can go to N4 or N0 (N0 is terminal). From N4, you can go to N3 or N0 (both terminal). N7 leads to N6 or N8; N6 leads to N9 (terminal), N8 is terminal. All paths eventually reach a terminal node. Since the player who cannot move loses, we want to force the opponent into a terminal node on their turn. The key is to avoid giving the opponent a move that leads to a winning path. From N1, moving to N7 leads to N6 or N8 — both terminal on the next turn. If the opponent moves to N7, they go to N6 or N8, both terminal. So if we go to N7, the opponent has no legal move after that. Thus, N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal first move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N7 or N5. Moving to N5 leads to N2 or N4. From N2, you can go to N4 or N0 (N0 is terminal). From N4, you can go to N3 or N0 (both terminal). N7 leads to N6 or N8; N6 leads to N9 (terminal), N8 is terminal. All paths eventually reach a terminal node. Since the player who cannot move loses, we want to force the opponent into a terminal node on their turn. The key is to avoid giving the opponent a move that leads to a winning path. From N1, moving to N7 leads to N6 or N8 — both terminal on the next turn. If the opponent moves to N7, they go to N6 or N8, both terminal. So if we go to N7, the opponent has no legal move after that. Thus, N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal first move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N6\nN8 -> N4, N6\nN9 -> N8, N3\nN4 -> N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N0\nN1 -> N4, N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N9 or N0. Moving to N0 leads directly to terminal (win for opponent if they can't move), so that would be a losing move. Moving to N9 allows reaching N8, then to N4 or N6. From N4, go to N2, then to N7 (terminal) or N6 (terminal). All paths from N9 eventually lead to terminal. However, since the game ends when a player cannot move, and all paths from any node eventually reach terminal, the key is to force the opponent into a position with no moves. From N5, moving to N9 leads to a position where the opponent can then move to N8, then to N4 or N6, and eventually terminal. But if we move to N0, we win immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, N0 is a terminal move — the opponent loses immediately. Therefore, choosing N0 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N9 or N0. Moving to N0 leads directly to terminal (win for opponent if they can't move), so that would be a losing move. Moving to N9 allows reaching N8, then to N4 or N6. From N4, go to N2, then to N7 (terminal) or N6 (terminal). All paths from N9 eventually lead to terminal. However, since the game ends when a player cannot move, and all paths from any node eventually reach terminal, the key is to force the opponent into a position with no moves. From N5, moving to N9 leads to a position where the opponent can then move to N8, then to N4 or N6, and eventually terminal. But if we move to N0, we win immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, N0 is a terminal move — the opponent loses immediately. Therefore, choosing N0 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}]
