en along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N1, N4\nN7 -> N6, N8\nN2 -> N4, N0, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N9\nN4 -> N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N6 or N8. Moving to N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who made the move), so that is a winning move. Moving to N8 allows further moves: N8 → N1 or N4. N1 and N4 both lead to terminal. Thus, from N8, the opponent is forced to move to terminal, meaning the current player wins. However, moving directly to N6 ends the game immediately with a win. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal as it ends the game on the current player's turn, securing a win.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N6 or N8. Moving to N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who made the move), so that is a winning move. Moving to N8 allows further moves: N8 → N1 or N4. N1 and N4 both lead to terminal. Thus, from N8, the opponent is forced to move to terminal, meaning the current player wins. However, moving directly to N6 ends the game immediately with a win. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal as it ends the game on the current player's turn, securing a win.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly: