7 (terminal) or N1. N7 is terminal, so N5 can force a win via N7. N1 leads to N3→N9 (terminal) or N0 (terminal), so N1 is also a winning move. Thus, N5 is a winning move.  \n- N2 → N1 → leads to N3→N9 or N0 (both terminal), so N2 is also a winning move.  \nAll three moves lead to winning positions. However, we must find the one that maximizes the outcome. Since all are winning, we pick the one that avoids giving the opponent a strong counter. N4 leads directly to N6 → terminal, a clean win. N5 and N2 branch into more complex paths but also win. However, N4 is shortest and most direct. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N1, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N5, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9\nN2 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, N6 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, N6 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}]
