[2026-08-13 21:57:58] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 228008] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N8\nN9 -> N3, N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N2\nN2 -> N8, N7\nN4 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N1 or N8. N8 leads to N3, which is terminal, so the next player has no move and wins. Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N1 leads to a subtree where the opponent can respond with N4 or N2. N4 leads to N9 or N0 (both terminal), or to N9 which leads to N3 or N6 (N6 terminal, N3 terminal). N2 leads to N8 or N7 (both terminal). All paths from N1 eventually end in terminal nodes, and the opponent can force a win. Thus, moving to N8 is optimal and ends the game immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N1 or N8. N8 leads to N3, which is terminal, so the next player has no move and wins. Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N1 leads to a subtree where the opponent can respond with N4 or N2. N4 leads to N9 or N0 (both terminal), or to N9 which leads to N3 or N6 (N6 terminal, N3 terminal). N2 leads to N8 or N7 (both terminal). All paths from N1 eventually end in terminal nodes, and the opponent can force a win. Thus, moving to N8 is optimal and ends the game immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N8\nN9 -> N3, N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N2\nN2 -> N8, N7\nN4 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Therefore, N3 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Therefore, N3 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N2, N5\nN8 -> N4, N3\nN3 -> N7, N0\nN9 -> N3, N1, N6\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N3, N1, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N3, N1, or N6. We analyze each:  \n- N3 leads to N7 or N0; N7 is terminal (win for next player), N0 leads to N4 (terminal). So from N3, opponent wins if they play to N7, but if we go to N0, then to N4, opponent loses. But N3 gives opponent a choice, so it's not clear.  \n- N1 leads to N8; from N8, opponent can go to N4 or N3. Both N4 and N3 eventually lead to terminal states, so opponent can force a win.  \n- N6 leads to N2 or N5; both are terminal. So from N6, the next player has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N6 forces the opponent to face a terminal state immediately — they lose. Hence, N6 is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N3, N1, or N6. We analyze each:  \n- N3 leads to N7 or N0; N7 is terminal (win for next player), N0 leads to N4 (terminal). So from N3, opponent wins if they play to N7, but if we go to N0, then to N4, opponent loses. But N3 gives opponent a choice, so it's not clear.  \n- N1 leads to N8; from N8, opponent can go to N4 or N3. Both N4 and N3 eventually lead to terminal states, so opponent can force a win.  \n- N6 leads to N2 or N5; both are terminal. So from N6, the next player has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N6 forces the opponent to face a terminal state immediately — they lose. Hence, N6 is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N2, N5\nN8 -> N4, N3\nN3 -> N7, N0\nN9 -> N3, N1, N6\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N5. Both N2 and N5 lead to terminal nodes (N2 → terminal, N5 → terminal), so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever move is chosen, the current player wins. Since both options result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N5 is valid and terminal. Therefore, the best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N5. Both N2 and N5 lead to terminal nodes (N2 → terminal, N5 → terminal), so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever move is chosen, the current player wins. Since both options result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N5 is valid and terminal. Therefore, the best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N11\nN11 -> N12, N6\nN3 -> N13, N6, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N7, N12, N9\nN13 -> N1\nN9 -> N8, N4\nN4 -> N8, N10\nN7 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N13, N6, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N13, N6, or N0. N13 leads to N1 (terminal), so the player who moves to N13 wins immediately. N6 leads to N2, N7, N12, N9 — all of which eventually lead to terminals, and the opponent can respond with moves that force a win. N0 leads to N11, then to N12 or N6. N12 is terminal, so if the opponent moves to N12, they win. But from N11, if the player moves to N6, the game continues. However, N13 is an immediate win: the current player moves to N1 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, choosing N13 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N13, N6, or N0. N13 leads to N1 (terminal), so the player who moves to N13 wins immediately. N6 leads to N2, N7, N12, N9 — all of which eventually lead to terminals, and the opponent can respond with moves that force a win. N0 leads to N11, then to N12 or N6. N12 is terminal, so if the opponent moves to N12, they win. But from N11, if the player moves to N6, the game continues. However, N13 is an immediate win: the current player moves to N1 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, choosing N13 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N11\nN11 -> N12, N6\nN3 -> N13, N6, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N7, N12, N9\nN13 -> N1\nN9 -> N8, N4\nN4 -> N8, N10\nN7 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the only legal move is to N1, which is a terminal node. Thus, the current player wins immediately by moving to N1. No further moves are possible, and the opponent has no legal move. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the only legal move is to N1, which is a terminal node. Thus, the current player wins immediately by moving to N1. No further moves are possible, and the opponent has no legal move. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N8, N6\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0, N3\nN11 -> N1, N0\nN6 -> N5, N11\nN8 -> N9, N13\nN13 -> N4, N10\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N4\nN1 -> N2, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N12, the player can move to N8 or N6. We analyze the outcomes of each move using backward induction from terminal nodes. Terminal nodes are losing (no moves). From N8: moves to N9 (terminal) or N13. N13 leads to N4 (terminal) or N10 (terminal). Both are losing. So N8 leads to a losing position for the next player, meaning N8 is a winning move. From N6: moves to N5 or N11. N5 leads to N9 or N4 (both terminal). N11 leads to N1 or N0. N1 leads to N2 or N7; N2 leads to N0 or N3; N3 leads to terminal. All paths eventually end. But N11 leads to N0 (terminal) or N1 → N2 → N0 or N3 → terminal — all terminal. So both N5 and N11 lead to positions where opponent wins eventually. Thus, N6 is a losing move. Therefore, choosing N8 is better. So the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N12, the player can move to N8 or N6. We analyze the outcomes of each move using backward induction from terminal nodes. Terminal nodes are losing (no moves). From N8: moves to N9 (terminal) or N13. N13 leads to N4 (terminal) or N10 (terminal). Both are losing. So N8 leads to a losing position for the next player, meaning N8 is a winning move. From N6: moves to N5 or N11. N5 leads to N9 or N4 (both terminal). N11 leads to N1 or N0. N1 leads to N2 or N7; N2 leads to N0 or N3; N3 leads to terminal. All paths eventually end. But N11 leads to N0 (terminal) or N1 → N2 → N0 or N3 → terminal — all terminal. So both N5 and N11 lead to positions where opponent wins eventually. Thus, N6 is a losing move. Therefore, choosing N8 is better. So the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N8, N6\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0, N3\nN11 -> N1, N0\nN6 -> N5, N11\nN8 -> N9, N13\nN13 -> N4, N10\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N4\nN1 -> N2, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N9 or N13. N9 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately. N13 leads to N4 or N10, both of which are terminal, so moving to N13 results in the opponent winning immediately. Thus, moving to N9 is the only winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "ep