his is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N11 or N0. Evaluating each:  \n- Moving to N11: leads to N8 or N13.  \n  - N8 → N7 or N3; N7 → N5 or N4; N4 → N10 or N6; N6 → N10 or N9 (all terminal or lead to terminal).  \n  - N13 → N5 or N1; N1 → terminal.  \n  All paths from N11 eventually reach terminal nodes, and since the game ends when a player cannot move, the player who forces the opponent into a terminal node wins.  \n  But more importantly, from N11, the opponent can move to terminal nodes or force further play. However, paths from N11 lead to nodes with limited depth and many terminal paths.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N12 or N9; both are terminal. So the opponent has no legal move after that — immediate win.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 immediately ends the game on the opponent's turn, so the first player wins. This is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N8, N13\nN13 -> N5, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N3\nN7 -> N5, N4\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> N11, N0\nN6 -> N10, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N10, N6\nN0 -> N12, N9\nN12 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N12, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N12 or N9. Both N12 and N9 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever move is chosen, the current player wins. Since both options end the game immediately, either is a winning move. Choosing N9 is safe and direct; however, N12 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent in outcome. Selecting N9 is sufficient and optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N12 or N9. Both N12 and N9 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever move is chosen, the current player wins. Since both options end the game immediately, either is a winning move. Choosing N9 is safe and direct; however, N12 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent in outcome. Selecting N9 is sufficient and optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}]
