xt move. Therefore, choosing N8 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N3, N4\nN1 -> N7\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8, N4, N6\nN6 -> N1\nN4 -> N0, N9\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N3 or N4. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N3 or N4. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN6 -> N1, N9\nN8 -> N4, N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N2\nN3 -> N7, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N1 or N9. N1 leads to N3 or N0; N3 leads to N7 (terminal) or N8, and N8 leads to N4 or N5 (both terminal). N0 leads to N9 or N2 (N2 is terminal). N9 leads to N5 (terminal). So paths from N1 or N9 eventually reach terminal nodes. The key is to avoid giving the opponent a winning path. Since all paths eventually lead to terminal nodes, the player who cannot move loses. From N6, moving to N1 or N9 both result in a forced chain ending in terminal nodes. However, moving to N9 leads directly to N5, terminal, so the opponent cannot move after that. But from N1, the game branches more. The critical point: if you go to N1, opponent can go to N3 → N7 or N8 → N4/N5, both terminal, so opponent can end it. But if you go to N9 → N5 → terminal, opponent has no move. So going to N9 ends the game immediately. Therefore, going to N9 is a winning move. Thus, optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N1 or N9. N1 leads to N3 or N0; N3 leads to N7 (terminal) or N8, and N8 leads to N4 or N5 (both terminal). N0 leads to N9 or N2 (N2 is terminal). N9 leads to N5 (terminal). So paths from N1 or N9 eventually reach terminal nodes. The key is to avoid giving the opponent a winning path. Since all paths eventually lead to terminal nodes, the player who cannot move loses. From N6, moving to N1 or N9 both result in a forced chain ending in terminal nodes. However, moving to N9 leads directly to N5, terminal, so the opponent cannot move after that. But from N1, the game branches more. The critical point: if you go to N1, opponent can go to N3 → N7 or N8 → N4/N5, both terminal, so opponent can end it. But if you go to N9 → N5 → terminal, opponent has no move. So going to N9 ends the game immediately. Therefore, going to N9 is a winning move. Thus, optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nT