<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N4. N4 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N4 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move from N9 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N9\nN8 -> N2, N7\nN6 -> N8, N1\nN0 -> N3, N6, N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N6\n\nLegal moves: N3, N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N3, N6, or N4. We analyze each:  \n- N3 leads to N4 or N6. Both N4 and N6 lead to chains ending in terminal (via N9→N5 or N8→N2/N7). All are losing if opponent plays optimally.  \n- N6 leads to N8 or N1. N1 is terminal, so player wins immediately if they move to N1. But N8 leads to N2 or N7; both are terminal. So N6 allows immediate terminal move (to N1) — a winning move.  \n- N4 leads to N9 → N5 → terminal, which is a losing chain if opponent controls.  \n\nN6 allows immediate move to N1 (terminal), which ends the game immediately. Thus, moving to N6 wins the game. Therefore, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N3, N6, or N4. We analyze each:  \n- N3 leads to N4 or N6. Both N4 and N6 lead to chains ending in terminal (via N9→N5 or N8→N2/N7). All are