8\nN0 -> N3, N8\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N2 or N1. N1 leads to N7, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. N2 leads to N1 or N6. From N1, as above, it leads to terminal. From N6, it leads to N0 or N4; N4 is terminal, so if you go to N0, you reach N3 or N8, both of which eventually lead to terminal. The game will end eventually. However, the key is that any path from N5 leads to a terminal state in finite steps. Since all moves eventually end, the player who cannot move loses. The optimal play is to force the opponent into a losing position. From N5, moving to N1 leads to terminal immediately, so the current player wins. Thus, choosing N1 is a winning move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N2 or N1. N1 leads to N7, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. N2 leads to N1 or N6. From N1, as above, it leads to terminal. From N6, it leads to N0 or N4; N4 is terminal, so if you go to N0, you reach N3 or N8, both of which eventually lead to terminal. The game will end eventually. However, the key is that any path from N5 leads to a terminal state in finite steps. Since all moves eventually end, the player who cannot move loses. The optimal play is to force the opponent into a losing position. From N5, moving to N1 leads to terminal immediately, so the current player wins. Thus, choosing N1 is a winning move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N1\nN6 -> N0, N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N6\nN3 -> N9, N8\nN0 -> N3, N8\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 ends the game on the next player's turn, and the current player wins. No other move from N1 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 ends the game on the next player's turn, and the current player wins. No other move from N1 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> N10, N2, N1\nN4 -> N7, N13\nN3 -> N5\nN7 -> N13, N2, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN11 -> N12\nN6 -> terminal\nN13 -> N6, N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N4, N7\n\nLegal moves: N11, N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N11, N4, or N7. N11 leads to terminal directly. N4 leads to N7 or N13; N7 leads to N13, N2, or N8. N2 and N8 eventually lead to terminal or cycles. N13 leads to N6 (terminal) or N3 (to N5, terminal). All paths eventually reach terminal. Since the player who cannot move loses, we aim to force the opponent into a losing position. From N9, moving to N11 ends the game immediately (current player wins). Thus, choosing N11 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N11, N4, or N7. N11 leads to terminal directly. N4 leads to N7 or N13; N7 leads to N13, N2, or N8. N2 and N8 eventually lead to terminal or cycles. N13 leads to N6 (terminal) or N3 (to N5, terminal). All paths eventually reach terminal. Since the player who cannot move loses, we aim to force the opponent into a losing position. From N9, moving to N11 ends the game immediately (current player wins). Thus, choosing N11 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N11\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> N10, N2, N1\nN4 -> N7, N13\nN3 -> N5\nN7 -> N13, N2, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN11 -> N12\nN6 -> terminal\nN13 -> N6, N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N4, N7\n\nLegal moves: N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N11, the only legal move is to N12, which leads to terminal. Thus, the current player wins immediately. No further moves are possible. The outcome is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N11, the only legal move is to N12, which leads to terminal. Thus, the current player wins immediately. No further moves are possible. The outcome is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N9, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> N2, N8\nN3 -> N0, N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N4\n\nLegal moves: N0, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N0 or N5. Evaluating each:  \n- Move to N0: from N0, can go to N2 or N8.  \n  - N2 → N1 or N4 (both terminal).  \n  - N8 → N9 or N5.  \n    - N9 → terminal.  \n    - N5 → N6 or N9 (N6 → N7 → terminal).  \n    This leads to a branching path with no immediate loss; however, the game is finite and alternating.  \n- Move to N5: from N5 → N6 or N9.  \n  - N9 → terminal (immediate loss for next player).  \n  - N6 → N7 → terminal.  \n  This path ends quickly.  \n\nSince the player who cannot move loses, and terminal nodes are dead-ends, we want to force the opponent into a position where they have no move or where the game ends on their turn.  \n\nFrom N3 → N5 leads to paths that end in terminal states within 1–2 steps, limiting the opponent's options. N3 → N0 opens more options but gives more control to opponent.  \n\nBest choice: N5, as it restricts future paths and leads to faster conclusion. Thus, maximizing outcome means ending the game quickly on opponent's turn.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N0 or N5. Evaluating each:  \n- Move to N0: from N0, can go to N2 or N8.  \n  - N2 → N1 or N4 (both terminal).  \n  - N8 → N9 or N5.  \n    - N9 → terminal.  \n    - N5 → N6 or N9 (N6 → N7 → terminal).  \n    This leads to a branching path with no immediate loss; however, the game is finite and alternating.  \n- Move to N5: from N5 → N6 or N9.  \n  - N9 → terminal (immediate loss for next player).  \n  - N6 → N7 → terminal.  \n  This path ends quickly.  \n\nSince the player who cannot move loses, and terminal nodes are dead-ends, we want to force the opponent into a position where they have no move or where the game ends on their turn.  \n\nFrom N3 → N5 leads to paths that end in terminal states within 1–2 steps, limiting the opponent's options. N3 → N0 opens more options but gives more control to opponent.  \n\nBest choice: N5, as it restricts future paths and leads to faster conclusion. Thus, maximizing outcome means ending the game quickly on opponent's turn.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N9, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> N2, N8\nN3 -> N0, N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N4\n\nLegal moves: N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N9. N9 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N9. N9 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately. Thus, movi