rther moves. However, choosing N7 leads to a terminal state in one move (via N13 or N5), and N5 leads to terminal states. Since all are losing positions for the opponent, both are equivalent. But since N7 directly leads to terminal via N13, it is a stronger immediate win. Thus, choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> terminal\nN0 -> N10, N7\nN8 -> N11, N1\nN10 -> N4, N9\nN7 -> N13, N5\nN4 -> N12, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N12\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N6\nN9 -> N6, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N13, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N13 (terminal) or N5. Moving to N13 ends the game immediately, and the current player wins. Thus, choosing N13 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N13 (terminal) or N5. Moving to N13 ends the game immediately, and the current player wins. Thus, choosing N13 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}]
