[2026-08-13 22:43:27] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 228008] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N4, N9\nN1 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N9\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N1\nN6 -> N2, N3\n\nLegal moves: N2, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N3.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 → terminal (win for next player, so loss for current)  \n  - N9 → N5 → terminal (loss for next player, so win for current) → so N2 can lead to a winning position via N9.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N7 (terminal) or N9.  \n  - N7 → terminal → loss for next player → win for current.  \n  - N9 → N5 → terminal → loss for next player → win for current.  \nSo both N2 and N3 lead to immediate terminal states via either N7 or N5. But critical: from N3, either move ends the game immediately (next player loses). Thus, N3 is a winning move, and gives immediate end of game (next player loses). Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal. The player who moves to N3 wins immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N3.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 → terminal (win for next player, so loss for current)  \n  - N9 → N5 → terminal (loss for next player, so win for current) → so N2 can lead to a winning position via N9.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N7 (terminal) or N9.  \n  - N7 → terminal → loss for next player → win for current.  \n  - N9 → N5 → terminal → loss for next player → win for current.  \nSo both N2 and N3 lead to immediate terminal states via either N7 or N5. But critical: from N3, either move ends the game immediately (next player loses). Thus, N3 is a winning move, and gives immediate end of game (next player loses). Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal. The player who moves to N3 wins immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N4, N9\nN1 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N9\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N1\nN6 -> N2, N3\n\nLegal moves: N7, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N7 or N9. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N5, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N7 or N9. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N5, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN0 -> N4, N1\nN3 -> N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N7\nN7 -> N0, N9\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N3 leads to N5, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Moving to N7 leads to N0 or N9. From N0: to N4 or N1 (both eventually lead to terminal). From N9: to N2 or N8 (both eventually lead to terminal). All these paths eventually end in terminal nodes. However, moving directly to N3 (to N5) ends the game immediately. Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal as it forces the opponent to lose immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N3 leads to N5, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Moving to N7 leads to N0 or N9. From N0: to N4 or N1 (both eventually lead to terminal). From N9: to N2 or N8 (both eventually lead to terminal). All these paths eventually end in terminal nodes. However, moving directly to N3 (to N5) ends the game immediately. Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal as it forces the opponent to lose immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN0 -> N4, N1\nN3 -> N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N7\nN7 -> N0, N9\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N5 ends the game on the next player's turn, and the current player wins. No other move is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N5 ends the game on the next player's turn, and the current player wins. No other move is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N9, N2\nN0 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN7 -> N1, N4\nN4 -> N6, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N1, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N1 or N4. N1 leads to N9 or N2, both of which are terminal (win for the player who moves there). N4 leads to N6 or N0. N6 leads to N5 or N0; N5 is terminal. N0 leads to N8 or N3; N3 is terminal. From N8, you go to N3 (terminal). Thus, any path from N4 eventually leads into terminal nodes, and the player who moves to a terminal node wins. Since both N1 and N4 eventually lead to terminal nodes, the first player to move wins. However, the goal is to maximize outcome, meaning choosing a move that forces the opponent into a losing position. Moving to N1 immediately leads to a terminal node (either N9 or N2), so the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N1 ends the game immediately and wins the game. Therefore, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N1 or N4. N1 leads to N9 or N2, both of which are terminal (win for the player who moves there). N4 leads to N6 or N0. N6 leads to N5 or N0; N5 is terminal. N0 leads to N8 or N3; N3 is terminal. From N8, you go to N3 (terminal). Thus, any path from N4 eventually leads into terminal nodes, and the player who moves to a terminal node wins. Since both N1 and N4 eventually lead to terminal nodes, the first player to move wins. However, the goal is to maximize outcome, meaning choosing a move that forces the opponent into a losing position. Moving to N1 immediately leads to a terminal node (either N9 or N2), so the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N1 ends the game immediately and wins the game. Therefore, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N9, N2\nN0 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN7 -> N1, N4\nN4 -> N6, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N9, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N2. Both N9 and N2 lead directly to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever of N9 or N2 is chosen ends the game on the next move. Since both are winning moves, either is optimal. Choosing N9 or N2 results in an immediate win. Both are valid, but selecting one is sufficient. We pick N2 as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N2. Both N9 and N2 lead directly to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever of N9 or N2 is chosen ends the game on the next move. Since both are winning moves, either is optimal. Choosing N9 or N2 results in an immediate win. Both are valid, but selecting one is sufficient. We pick N2 as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N9, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N1, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N0\nN8 -> N3, N7\nN4 -> N7\nN3 -> N2, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N9 or N8. Evaluating terminal states and game outcomes:  \n- N9 leads to N1 or N8:  \n   - N1 → N4 or N0; N4 → N7 (terminal), N0 → terminal.  \n     Path N1→N4→N7: chain ends, opponent wins after you move to N7.  \n     Path N1→N0: opponent wins immediately.  \n     N1→N4: leads to N7, opponent wins.  \n   - N8 → N3 or N7:  \n     - N3 → N2 or N6; both terminal.  \n     - N7 → terminal.  \n     So from N8, paths go to terminal quickly.  \n- N8 has direct path to N7, which is terminal.  \n- From N5 → N9: leads to branching paths that may result in long games but ultimately end with player losing if opponent plays well.  \n- From N5 → N8: leads to immediate terminal options (N3 or N7), and since N7 is terminal, the opponent has no move and wins immediately after your move.  \nTherefore, moving to N8 leads to a loss immediately for the opponent, as they can’t move. Hence, N8 is a winning move. The analysis shows N8 leads to a forced win. Thus, optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N9 or N8. Evaluating terminal states and game outcomes:  \n- N9 leads to N1 or N8:  \n   - N1 → N4 or N0; N4 → N7 (terminal), N0 → terminal.  \n     Path N1→N4→N7: chain ends, opponent wins after you move to N7.  \n     Path N1→N0: opponent wins immediately.  \n     N1→N4: leads to N7, opponent wins.  \n   - N8 → N3 or N7:  \n     - N3 → N2 or N6; both terminal.  \n     - N7 → terminal.  \n     So from N8, paths go to terminal quickly.  \n- N8 has direct path to N7, which is terminal.  \n- From N5 → N9: leads to branching paths that may result in long games but ultimately end with player losing if opponent plays well.  \n- From N5 → N8: leads to immediate terminal options (N3 or N7), and since N7 is terminal, the opponent has no move and wins immediately after your move.  \nTherefore, moving to N8 leads to a loss immediately for the opponent, as they can’t move. Hence, N8 is a winning move. The analysis shows N8 leads to a forced win. Thus, optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N9, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N1, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N0\nN8 -> N3, N7\nN4 -> N7\nN3 -> N2, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4, N8\nN0 -> N8, N6\nN3 -> N1, N9\nN8 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N0, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N4, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4 or N8. Analyze each:\n\n- Move to N4: Then from N4, possible moves are to N0 or N3.\n  - From N0: to N8 or N6; both lead to terminal or further chains.\n  - From N3: to N1 or N9; N1 leads to terminal, N9 leads to N1, which ends.\n  - Chains eventually reach terminals; no loops, so all paths end.\n- Move to N8: From N8, go to N2 or N7.\n  - N2 → terminal\n  - N7 → terminal\n  So from N8, the opponent gets no further move.\n\nFrom N5 → N8, the opponent faces terminal or immediate loss. Thus, N8 is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, choosing N8 leads to immediate end of game for opponent, maximizing outcome.\n\nFinal choice: N8</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4 or N8. Analyze each:\n\n- Move to N4: Then from N4, possible moves are to N0 or N3.\n  - From N0: to N8 or N6; both lead to terminal or further chains.\n  - From N3: to N1 or N9; N1 leads to terminal, N9 leads to N1, which ends.\n  - Chains eventually reach terminals; no loops, so all paths end.\n- Move to N8: From N8, go to N2 or N7.\n  - N2 → terminal\n  - N7 → terminal\n  So from N8, the opponent gets no further move.\n\nFrom N5 → N8, the opponent faces terminal or immediate loss. Thus, N8 is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, choosing N8 leads to immediate end of game for opponent, maximizing outcome.\n\nFinal choice: N8</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4, N8\nN0 -> N8, N6\nN3 -> N1, N9\nN8 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N0, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N2, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N7. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. N7 also leads to terminal, so moving to N7 also ends the game immediately. Both moves result in the opponent having no move. Thus, either move wins. Since both are terminal moves, either is optimal. Choosing N2 is valid and ends the game immediately. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N7. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. N7 also leads to terminal, so moving to N7 also ends the game immediately. Both moves result in the opponent having no move. Thus, either move wins. Since both are terminal moves, either is optimal. Choosing N2 is valid and ends the game immediately. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}]
