[2026-08-13 23:08:22] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 228008] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N3\nN5 -> N6, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N8, N4\nN8 -> N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N1\n\nLegal moves: N8, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N8 or N4. N4 leads to N9 (terminal) or N3 (leads to terminal), so any move from N4 ends in a terminal state. N8 leads to N5, which leads to N6 or N7. N7 goes to terminal, so it's a winning move. N6 leads to N2 or N1; N2 is terminal, N1 leads to N9 or N7, both terminal. So any path from N5 eventually ends. From N0 → N8 → N5 → N7 ends the game immediately with the player to move losing. But if the opponent is forced into a losing move, we win. The key is that every path from N0 eventually leads into cycles or dead ends with terminal nodes. The structure is such that all paths eventually reach terminal nodes, so the first player (current player) can win by forcing the opponent into a losing state. The best move from N0 is to N4, which leads directly to terminal options. N4 → N3 or N9, both lead to terminal. Since the opponent has no move, the first player wins. So best choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N8 or N4. N4 leads to N9 (terminal) or N3 (leads to terminal), so any move from N4 ends in a terminal state. N8 leads to N5, which leads to N6 or N7. N7 goes to terminal, so it's a winning move. N6 leads to N2 or N1; N2 is terminal, N1 leads to N9 or N7, both terminal. So any path from N5 eventually ends. From N0 → N8 → N5 → N7 ends the game immediately with the player to move losing. But if the opponent is forced into a losing move, we win. The key is that every path from N0 eventually leads into cycles or dead ends with terminal nodes. The structure is such that all paths eventually reach terminal nodes, so the first player (current player) can win by forcing the opponent into a losing state. The best move from N0 is to N4, which leads directly to terminal options. N4 → N3 or N9, both lead to terminal. Since the opponent has no move, the first player wins. So best choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N3\nN5 -> N6, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N8, N4\nN8 -> N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N1\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N9 or N3. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N9 or N3. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN13 -> N7, N9, N6\nN12 -> N5, N2, N0\nN0 -> N11\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3, N0\nN10 -> terminal\nN7 -> N12, N4, N6\nN6 -> N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N10, N4\nN9 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N7, N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the player can move to N7, N9, or N6. We evaluate the outcomes of each move.  \n- N6 → N1 (terminal), so the next player cannot move, and the current player wins. This is a winning move.  \n- N9 → N8 → from N8: can go to N3 (terminal) or N0 → N0 leads to N11 → terminal, so all paths eventually terminate. From N9, the opponent gets to move from N8, and eventually, paths lead to terminal, so it's not immediate win.  \n- N7 → leads to N12 → N5/N2/N0; chains can go deep, but eventually all paths reach terminals. However, since N7 leads to branching, the opponent may have multiple options, but since the game is zero-sum and terminal on all branches, only immediate terminal wins matter.  \n\nOnly N6 directly leads to a terminal next move. Hence, choosing N6 ends the game immediately. Thus, N6 is optimal. </reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the player can move to N7, N9, or N6. We evaluate the outcomes of each move.  \n- N6 → N1 (terminal), so the next player cannot move, and the current player wins. This is a winning move.  \n- N9 → N8 → from N8: can go to N3 (terminal) or N0 → N0 leads to N11 → terminal, so all paths eventually terminate. From N9, the opponent gets to move from N8, and eventually, paths lead to terminal, so it's not immediate win.  \n- N7 → leads to N12 → N5/N2/N0; chains can go deep, but eventually all paths reach terminals. However, since N7 leads to branching, the opponent may have multiple options, but since the game is zero-sum and terminal on all branches, only immediate terminal wins matter.  \n\nOnly N6 directly leads to a terminal next move. Hence, choosing N6 ends the game immediately. Thus, N6 is optimal. </reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN13 -> N7, N9, N6\nN12 -> N5, N2, N0\nN0 -> N11\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3, N0\nN10 -> terminal\nN7 -> N12, N4, N6\nN6 -> N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N10, N4\nN9 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N1, which leads to terminal. Thus, the current player wins immediately. No further moves are needed. The optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N1, which leads to terminal. Thus, the current player wins immediately. No further moves are needed. The optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep y