[2026-08-13 23:41:28] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 228008] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N5, N6\nN1 -> N8, N2, N5\nN5 -> N3\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N8, N2, or N5. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N8: leads to subtree with branches to N7, N9. From N7, paths go to N0 (terminal), N5, N6; N5 → N3 (terminal), so path ends. N6 → N4 → terminal. All paths eventually end in terminal. From N9, terminal. This leads to a complex game tree with many options; it's a non-trivial win/loss state.\n\n- Move to N2: leads to N8 → then branches again. This is similar to N8.\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N3 → terminal. Immediate end of game after one step.\n\nIf you move to N5, the opponent cannot move from N3 and thus loses immediately. So this is a winning move (you force the opponent into a losing state). Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes outcome. Thus, optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N8, N2, or N5. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N8: leads to subtree with branches to N7, N9. From N7, paths go to N0 (terminal), N5, N6; N5 → N3 (terminal), so path ends. N6 → N4 → terminal. All paths eventually end in terminal. From N9, terminal. This leads to a complex game tree with many options; it's a non-trivial win/loss state.\n\n- Move to N2: leads to N8 → then branches again. This is similar to N8.\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N3 → terminal. Immediate end of game after one step.\n\nIf you move to N5, the opponent cannot move from N3 and thus loses immediately. So this is a winning move (you force the opponent into a losing state). Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes outcome. Thus, optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N5, N6\nN1 -> N8, N2, N5\nN5 -> N3\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N3 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN3 -> N6, N8\nN0 -> N9, N7\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N5\nN1 -> N0, N2\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N0 or N2.  \n- Move to N2: leads to N5, which is terminal → current player wins immediately.  \n- Move to N0: leads to either N9 or N7.  \n  - N9 → N4 or N3; both eventually lead to terminal paths.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N6; N6 is terminal.  \n  - All paths from N0 eventually reach terminal nodes via branching, but no immediate win.  \n\nThe move to N2 leads directly to terminal (N5), so the player wins immediately. This is a winning move. Thus, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N0 or N2.  \n- Move to N2: leads to N5, which is terminal → current player wins immediately.  \n- Move to N0: leads to either N9 or N7.  \n  - N9 → N4 or N3; both eventually lead to terminal paths.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N6; N6 is terminal.  \n  - All paths from N0 eventually reach terminal nodes via branching, but no immediate win.  \n\nThe move to N2 leads directly to terminal (N5), so the player wins immediately. This is a winning move. Thus, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN3 -> N6, N8\nN0 -> N9, N7\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N5\nN1 -> N0, N2\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N5 results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N2. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N5 results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N2. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N7\nN6 -> N7, N3\nN0 -> N6, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN4 -> N5, N0\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the player can move to either N5 or N0. N5 leads to N2 (terminal), so the next player cannot move and loses. Thus, moving to N5 ends the game immediately, winning the game. N0 leads to N6 or N3. From N6, paths go to N7 or N3; from N3, to N8 or N1. N1 and N7/N9 lead to terminal states, so chains may result in back-and-forth play. However, any such path eventually leads into cycles or terminal states. But the critical insight is: moving to N5 ends the game directly and wins immediately. Therefore, N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the player can move to either N5 or N0. N5 leads to N2 (terminal), so the next player cannot move and loses. Thus, moving to N5 ends the game immediately, winning the game. N0 leads to N6 or N3. From N6, paths go to N7 or N3; from N3, to N8 or N1. N1 and N7/N9 lead to terminal states, so chains may result in back-and-forth play. However, any such path eventually leads into cycles or terminal states. But the critical insight is: moving to N5 ends the game directly and wins immediately. Therefore, N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N7\nN6 -> N7, N3\nN0 -> N6, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN4 -> N5, N0\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N2. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move from N5 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N2. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move from N5 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N4, N6, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N3, N8\nN9 -> N0, N5\nN4 -> N9\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7\nN8 -> N1\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N4, N6, or N0. We evaluate the outcome of each move considering the game is a directed acyclic graph and the player who cannot move loses. \n\n- Move to N4: From N4, go to N9. From N9, you can go to N0 or N5. If to N5, terminal (opponent wins). If to N0, continue. N0 branches to N6, N3, N8. N3 and N8 end in terminal (lose if you're forced to go to terminal). So, the path is complex, but eventually leads to cycles or terminal nodes. However, from N6 → N7 (terminal), so from N6 it ends. This suggests many paths end in terminal states depending on opponent play.\n\n- Move to N6: From N6 → N7 (terminal). The next player cannot move — current player wins.\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, options are N6, N3, N8. N3 and N8 lead to terminals; N6 leads to N7. So opponent might win depending on play.\n\nBest move is to N6 directly: leads to terminal on next step — opponent loses immediately.\n\nTherefore, best move is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N4, N6, or N0. We evaluate the outcome of each move considering the game is a directed acyclic graph and the player who cannot move loses. \n\n- Move to N4: From N4, go to N9. From N9, you can go to N0 or N5. If to N5, terminal (opponent wins). If to N0, continue. N0 branches to N6, N3, N8. N3 and N8 end in terminal (lose if you're forced to go to terminal). So, the path is complex, but eventually leads to cycles or terminal nodes. However, from N6 → N7 (terminal), so from N6 it ends. This suggests many paths end in terminal states depending on opponent play.\n\n- Move to N6: From N6 → N7 (terminal). The next player cannot move — current player wins.\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, options are N6, N3, N8. N3 and N8 lead to terminals; N6 leads to N7. So opponent might win depending on play.\n\nBest move is to N6 directly: leads to terminal on next step — opponent loses immediately.\n\nTherefore, best move is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N4, N6, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N3, N8\nN9 -> N0, N5\nN4 -> N9\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7\nN8 -> N1\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N13\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N7\nN9 -> N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N3, N1\nN3 -> N12, N4\nN5 -> N9, N11, N3\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N11\nN12 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N4, N3, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N4, N3, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N4 leads to N0 or N13; both N0 (→ N2 or N11) and N13 (terminal) are weak. N11 leads to terminal; N0 leads to N2 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). So N4 is not strong.  \n- N3 leads to N12 or N4. N12 → N8 (terminal); N4 as above. Both paths are weak or terminal.  \n- N1 leads to N5, N7. N7 → terminal (winning move). N5 → N9 (→ terminal), N11 (→ terminal), N3 (→ N12 or N4). So from N1, a path to terminal exists quickly, and control is possible. Choosing N1 gives immediate access to a winning path. Therefore, N1 is best. From N1, the opponent faces N5 or N7 — both end in terminal or lose. The path to N7 is terminal immediately, so the first player wins. Thus, moving to N1 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N4, N3, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N4 leads to N0 or N13; both N0 (→ N2 or N11) and N13 (terminal) are weak. N11 leads to terminal; N0 leads to N2 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). So N4 is not strong.  \n- N3 leads to N12 or N4. N12 → N8 (terminal); N4 as above. Both paths are weak or terminal.  \n- N1 leads to N5, N7. N7 → terminal (winning move). N5 → N9 (→ terminal), N11 (→ terminal), N3 (→ N12 or N4). So from N1, a path to terminal exists quickly, and control is possible. Choosing N1 gives immediate access to a winning path. Therefore, N1 is best. From N1, the opponent faces N5 or N7 — both end in terminal or lose. The path to N7 is terminal immediately, so the first player wins. Thus, moving to N1 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N13\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N7\nN9 -> N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N3, N1\nN3 -> N12, N4\nN5 -> N9, N11, N3\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N11\nN12 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}]
