me: Move to N4 → terminal win. This is a direct and safe win. Thus, choosing N4 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N4, N1, or N7. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N4: leads to N8 → terminal → current player wins immediately (opponent loses). Immediate win.\n- Move to N1: leads to N5 → next player can go to N9, N6, or N7. N6 or N9 leads to terminal; N7 leads to loop (N7→N2 or N0). This path has risk of looping and may not end in immediate win. Not optimal.\n- Move to N7: leads to N2 or N0. N2 → terminal, so if you go N7→N2, opponent wins immediately. If go to N0, then to N6 → terminal. This is a losing move for opponent only if forced into terminal after your move.\n\nBest outcome: Move to N4 → terminal win. This is a direct and safe win. Thus, choosing N4 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N5\nN4 -> N8\nN5 -> N9, N6, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N1, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N8. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N8 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N8. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N8 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N6\nN0 -> N7, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N8\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N8. N8 leads to N2 or N6; N2 is terminal, so player who moves to N2 wins immediately. N6 leads to N5 or N0. N5 leads to N1, which leads to N4 (terminal), and N0 leads to N7 or N1. N7 is terminal. Thus, from N6, the path can continue, and from N8, immediate terminal option exists. Analyzing backwards, if a player moves to a node that leads to opponent's forced move or terminal, they win. From N8: one option is to N2 (terminal), so the player to move from N8 can win immediately. So, moving to N8 is a winning move. Therefore, optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N8. N8 leads to N2 or N6; N2 is terminal, so player who moves to N2 wins immediately. N6 leads to N5 or N0. N5 leads to N1, which leads to N4 (terminal), and N0 leads to N7 or N1. N7 is terminal. Thus, from N6, the path can continue, and from N8, immediate terminal option exists. Analyzing backwards, if a player moves to a node that leads to opponent's forced move or terminal, they win. From N8: one option is to N2 (terminal), so the player to move from N8 can win immediately. So, moving to N8 is a winning move. Therefore, optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N6\nN0 -> N7, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N8\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N6. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N6. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5\nN6 -> N3, N0\nN3 -> N1, N8\nN9 -> N2, N4\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N7\nN2 -> N6, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N2 or N4. Analyze each option:  \n- Move to N2: From N2, possible moves to N6 or N8.  \n   - N6 → N3 or N0 (N3 → N1 or N8; N0 terminal). All paths eventually reach terminal or cycles. But from N8 → N5 → terminal, and N3 → N1 → terminal, so no loop. N6 leads to end.  \n   - N8 → N5 → terminal.  \n   So both branches end in terminal; player who faces terminal loses. So player moving from N2 will allow opponent to move into end.  \n   Thus, if first player goes to N2, second player can play to terminal or avoid losing early.  \n- Move to N4: From N4 → N5 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Either move leads directly to terminal. So second player wins immediately.  \n\nTherefore, moving to N2 gives second player a chance to respond and control game flow. But both paths from N2 and N4 lead to terminal quickly. Since N4 leads to immediate terminal (next player loses), moving to N4 forces opponent to lose immediately — thus, optimal. Hence, choosing N4 leads to win.  \nThus, best move is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N2 or N4. Analyze each option:  \n- Move to N2: From N2, possible moves to N6 or N8.  \n   - N6 → N3 or N0 (N3 → N1 or N8; N0 terminal). All paths eventually reach terminal or cycles. But from N8 → N5 → terminal, and N3 → N1 → terminal, so no loop. N6 leads to end.  \n   - N8 → N5 → terminal.  \n   So both branches end in terminal; player who faces terminal loses. So player moving from N2 will allow opponent to move into end.  \n   Thus, if first player goes to N2, second player can play to terminal or avoid losing early.  \n- Move to N4: From N4 → N5 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Either move leads directly to terminal. So second player wins immediately.  \n\nTherefore, moving to N2 gives second player a chance to respond and control game flow. But both paths from N2 and N4 lead to terminal quickly. Since N4 leads to immediate terminal (next player loses), moving to N4 forces opponent to lose immediately — thus, optimal. Hence, choosing N4 leads to win.  \nThus, best move is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5\nN6 -> N3, N0\nN3 -> N1, N8\nN9 -> N2, N4\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N7\nN2 -> N6, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N5 or N7. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. N7 also leads to terminal, so moving to N7 also wins immediately. Both moves end the game immediately with a win for the current player. Thus, either move is optimal. Choosing N5 is valid and terminal. Therefore, the best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N5 or N7. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. N7 also leads to terminal, so moving to N7 also wins immediately. Both moves end the game immediately with a win for the current player. Thus, either move is optimal. Choosing N5 is valid and terminal. Therefore, the best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N13\nN3 -> N4, N9, N12\nN2 -> N5, N1, N10, N7\nN4 -> N9\nN13 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N10, N5, N8\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6\nN11 -> terminal\nN12 -> N0\nN9 -> N2, N11\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N9, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N4, N9, or N12. We analyze each option:  \n- N4 leads to N9. From N9, possible moves are to N2 or N11. N11 ends the game immediately, so the player who moves to N11 wins. N2 leads into a longer chain. This path may eventually result in a losing position due to deep recursion and possible loops.  \n- N9 leads to N2 or N11. N11 is terminal and ends the game. If a player moves to N11, they win immediately. But if opponent controls N2, they can continue. This is a branching path with high risk.  \n- N12 leads to N0, which is terminal. So the player moving to N12 immediately ends the game and wins. Thus, moving to N12 ends the game on the next move, giving the current player an immediate win.  \n\nThus, choosing N12 is optimal because it directly leads to a terminal node. The opponent has no move after that. Therefore, the best choice is N12.</reason>  \n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N4, N9, or N12. We analyze each option:  \n- N4 leads to N9. From N9, possible moves are to N2 or N11. N11 ends the game immediately, so the player who moves to N11 wins. N2 leads into a longer chain. This path may eventually result in a losing position due to deep recursion and possible loops.  \n- N9 leads to N2 or N11. N11 is terminal and ends the game. If a player moves to N11, they win immediately. But if opponent controls N2, they can continue. This is a branching path with high risk.  \n- N12 leads to N0, which is terminal. So the player moving to N12 immediately ends the game and wins. Thus, moving to N12 ends the game on the next move, giving the current player an immediate win.  \n\nThus, choosing N12 is optimal because it directly leads to a terminal node. The opponent has no move after that. Therefore, the best choice is N12.</reason>  \n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N13\nN3 -> N4, N9, N12\nN2 -> N5, N1, N10, N7\nN4 -> N9\nN13 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N10, N5, N8\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6\nN11 -> terminal\nN12 -> N0\nN9 -> N2, N11\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N12, the only legal move is to N0. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N0 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N12, the only legal move is to N0. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N0 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}]
