erminal). This creates paths ending in terminal, meaning the player who reaches a terminal node wins if no further moves are available.  \n- Moving to N2 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (terminal). Either way, the next player is forced to move to a terminal, and the current player (who just moved) wins.  \nThus, N2 leads to immediate terminal on the next move, meaning the opponent loses. Therefore, choosing N2 is a winning move. The optimal move from N4 is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N3, N2\nN2 -> N8, N1\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N8\nN3 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> N7\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N8 or N1. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately. N1 also leads to terminal, so moving to N1 also wins immediately. Both moves result in the opponent having no legal move. Thus, either move is a winning move. Since both are winning, either is optimal. Choosing N8 is valid and terminal-inducing.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N8 or N1. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately. N1 also leads to terminal, so moving to N1 also wins immediately. Both moves result in the opponent having no legal move. Thus, either move is a winning move. Since both are winning, either is optimal. Choosing N8 is valid and terminal-inducing.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N6, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N4\nN7 -> N0, N1\nN1 -> N2\nN0 -> N5, N9\n\nLegal moves: N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N0 or N1. Analyzing each move:  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N5 or N9. N5 leads to N3 or N8 (both terminal paths); N3 leads to N1 or N4 (N4 terminal, N1 leads to N2 terminal). N9 leads to N6 or N4 (both terminal). All paths eventually reach terminal, so N0 leads to a chain ending with opponent's loss if no cycles.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N2 (terminal). The player who moves to N1 immediately forces a terminal path (N1 → N2), so the next player loses. This is a terminal move.  \n\nThe critical insight: the game is impartial and zero-sum; the goal is to force opponent into a losing position. Moving to N1 ends immediately in a loss for the next player, thus winning the game. This is optimal. So, choosing N1 maximizes outcome.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N0 or N1. Analyzing each move:  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N5 or N9. N5 leads to N3 or N8 (both terminal paths); N3 leads to N1 or N4 (N4 terminal, N1 leads to N2 terminal). N9 leads to N6 or N4 (both terminal). All paths eventually reach terminal, so N0 leads to a chain ending with opponent's loss if no cycles.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N2 (terminal). The p