leads to recursive chains; however, N6 → N7 ends the game immediately, so it is a terminal win for the player who lands there. Thus, choosing N6 ends in opponent’s turn with no move. The optimal play is to go to N6, which leads directly to a forced terminal win in the next move. Hence, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N0, N9, N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7\nN4 -> N6, N2, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8, N6\nN2 -> N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 ends the game on the next player's turn, and the current player wins. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 ends the game on the next player's turn, and the current player wins. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N3\nN3 -> N6, N0\nN8 -> N2\nN1 -> N8, N9, N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N9, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N8, N9, or N5. N8 leads to N2 (terminal), so the opponent cannot move — the current player wins immediately. N9 leads to N4 or N7; N7 is terminal, but N4 leads into longer chains. N5 leads to N9, which is recursive and leads to possible backtracking. However, any move to N8 (direct to terminal) ends the game immediately with the opponent losing. Thus, moving to N8 is optimal and guarantees a win. Therefore, the best choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N8, N9, or N5. N8 leads to N2 (terminal), so the opponent cannot move — the current player wins immediately. N9 leads to N4 or N7; N7 is terminal, but N4 leads into longer chains. N5 leads to N9, which is recursive and leads to possible backtracking. However, any move to N8 (direct to terminal) ends the game immediately with the opponent losing. Thus, moving to N8 is optimal and guarantees a win. Therefore, the best choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N3\nN3 -> N6, N0\nN8 -> N2\nN1 -> N8, N9, N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N7\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the only legal move is to N2, which leads to terminal. Thus, the current player wins immediately. No further moves are needed. The optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the only legal move is to N2, which leads to terminal. Thus, the current player wins immediately. No further moves are needed. The optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N8, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N9\nN6 -> N4, N0\nN0 -> N1, N7\nN4 -> N2\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N9. Analyze each option:\n\n- Move to N6: From N6, options are to N4 or N0.\n  - N4 → N2 → terminal (win for next player, so this is losing if you can't continue).\n  - N0 → N1 (terminal) or N7 → (N1 or N5). N1 and N5 are terminal. So path leads to terminal after few steps. But eventually, it's a chain where the opponent will win or lose depending on parity.\n  - Path from N6 to N0 to N7 to N5 (terminal) or N1 → terminal. Each move leads to terminal in 2–3 steps. It's a short path and eventually, the first player to be blocked loses. Since all are terminal paths, the player who makes the last move wins.\n\n- Move to N9: From N9 → N8 (terminal), so the next player is forced to move to terminal and loses immediately.\n\nThus, if we go to N9, opponent moves to N8 and loses. That's a win immediately.\n\nSo choosing N9 leads to an immediate loss for the opponent.\n\nTherefore, best choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N9. Analyze each option:\n\n- Move to N6: From N6, options are to N4 or N0.\n  - N4 → N2 → terminal (win for next player, so this is losing if you can't continue).\n  - N0 → N1 (terminal) or N7 → (N1 or N5). N1 and N5 are terminal. So path leads to terminal after few steps. But eventually, it's a chain where the opponent will win or lose depending on parity.\n  - Path from N6 to N0 to N7 to N5 (terminal) or N1 → terminal. Each move leads to terminal in 2–3 steps. It's a short path and eventually, the first player to be blocked loses. Since all are terminal paths, the player who makes the last move wins.\n\n- Move to N9: From N9 → N8 (terminal), so the next player is forced to move to terminal and loses immediately.\n\nThus, if we go to N9, opponent moves to N8 and loses. That's a win immediately.\n\nSo choosing N9 leads to an immediate loss for the opponent.\n\nTherefore, best choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N8, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N9\nN6 -> N4, N0\nN0 -> N1, N7\nN4 -> N2\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N4. N8 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N8 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N4. N8 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N8 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2\nN6 -> N5, N7, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> N5, N6, N7\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N3\nN7 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N5, N6, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5, N6, or N7. We evaluate the outcome of each move using backward induction:\n\n- N5 leads to N8, then to terminal → the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (since the opponent cannot move after terminal). So N5 is a winning move.\n- N6 leads to N5, N7, or N4:\n  - N5 → N8 → terminal → opponent wins after N8\n  - N7 → N0 → N9 or N3 → N3 → N2 → terminal; N9 → terminal → opponent wins\n  - N4 → terminal → opponent wins\n  → All are terminal paths; the opponent eventually wins. This is not good.\n- N7 leads to N0 → N9 (terminal) or N3 → N2 (terminal). Either way, opponent wins after next move.\n\nSo only N5 leads to a situation where the opponent faces a terminal node after a single move, meaning the current player wins immediately.\n\nHence, choosing N5 leads to immediate terminal path after opponent's turn. All other paths let opponent win eventually.\n\nOptimal choice: N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5, N6, or N7. We evaluate the outcome of each move using backward induction:\n\n- N5 leads to N8, then to terminal → the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (since the opponent cannot move after terminal). So N5 is a winning move.\n- N6 leads to N5, N7, or N4:\n  - N5 → N8 → terminal → opponent wins after N8\n  - N7 → N0 → N9 or N3 → N3 → N2 → terminal; N9 → terminal → opponent wins\n  - N4 → terminal → opponent wins\n  → All are terminal paths; the opponent eventually wins. This is not good.\n- N7 leads to N0 → N9 (terminal) or N3 → N2 (terminal). Either way, opponent wins after next move.\n\nSo only N5 leads to a situation where the opponent faces a terminal node after a single move, meaning the current player wins immediately.\n\nHence, choosing N5 leads to immediate terminal path after opponent's turn. All other paths let opponent win eventually.\n\nOptimal choice: N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2\nN6 -> N5, N7, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> N5, N6, N7\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N3\nN7 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N8, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N8 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Therefore, moving to N8 is the only optimal choice. </reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N8, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N8 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Therefore, moving to N8 is the only optimal choice. </reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN5 -> N1, N0\nN7 -> N6, N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N2\nN8 -> N3, N4\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to either N6 or N5. \n\n- Moving to N5 leads to N1 or N0. N1 → terminal, N0 → terminal; both are terminal, so the opponent will lose immediately. So this is a winning move.\n- Moving to N6 leads to either N9 or N8. \n  - N9 → N0 (terminal), so next player loses. \n  - N8 → N3 or N4: \n    - N3 → terminal, N4 → N9 or N2; N9 → N0, N2 → terminal. This leads to a long path, but eventually results in terminal states, and the chain will end with a win for the first player eventually.\n  However, the key is to minimize opponent's options. But both N5 and N6 end in paths to terminals. \n\nBut crucially, from N5: either N1 or N0, both terminal, so the opponent gets no move. So if player moves to N5, the opponent has no legal move and loses immediately. That is a winning move. \n\nThus, the best choice is N5. The move to N5 ensures immediate end of the game on opponent's turn.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to either N6 or N5. \n\n- Moving to N5 leads to N1 or N0. N1 → terminal, N0 → terminal; both are terminal, so the opponent will lose immediately. So this is a winning move.\n- Moving to N6 leads to either N9 or N8. \n  - N9 → N0 (terminal), so next player loses. \n  - N8 → N3 or N4: \n    - N3 → terminal, N4 → N9 or N2; N9 → N0, N2 → terminal. This leads to a long path, but eventually results in terminal states, and the chain will end with a win for the first player eventually.\n  However, the key is to minimize opponent's options. But both N5 and N6 end in paths to terminals. \n\nBut crucially, from N5: either N1 or N0, both terminal, so the opponent gets no move. So if player moves to N5, the opponent has no legal move and loses immediately. That is a winning move. \n\nThus, the best choice is N5. The move to N5 ensures immediate end of the game on opponent's turn.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN5 -> N1, N0\nN7 -> N6, N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N2\nN8 -> N3, N4\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N1 or N0. N1 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. N0 also leads to terminal, so moving to N0 also wins immediately. Either move ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Thus, both N1 and N0 are winning moves. Since both result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N0 is valid and terminal-secure. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N1 or N0. N1 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. N0 also leads to terminal, so moving to N0 also wins immediately. Either move ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Thus, both N1 and N0 are winning moves. Since both result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N0 is valid and terminal-secure. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}]
