maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N0\nN3 -> N2\nN7 -> N8, N4\nN8 -> N6, N3\nN1 -> N8, N7\nN9 -> N1, N5\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. Both N2 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, so whichever is chosen ends the game immediately. Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to either N2 or N0 ends the game on the next player's turn. Thus, the current player wins by making a move. Both N2 and N0 are terminal, so either is a winning move. Choosing N0 is valid and safe; however, since both end the game immediately, any choice is equivalent. We select one. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. Both N2 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, so whichever is chosen ends the game immediately. Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to either N2 or N0 ends the game on the next player's turn. Thus, the current player wins by making a move. Both N2 and N0 are terminal, so either is a winning move. Choosing N0 is valid and safe; however, since both end the game immediately, any choice is equivalent. We select one. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N0\nN3 -> N1, N4\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN6 -> N9, N8\n\nLegal moves: N3, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N3 or N0. If the player moves to N0, the opponent faces N0 and can move to N5 or N6. From N5, the opponent moves to N2 (terminal), ending the game on the next turn. From N6, the opponent moves to either N9 or N8, both leading to paths eventually ending at terminals. If the first player chooses N3, the opponent can go to N1 (terminal) or N4. N4 leads to terminal, so N1 or N4 end the game immediately. From N3, the opponent has two options, but either leads to a terminal or recursive paths. The best move from N7 is to N3 because it gives immediate control and leads to terminal or a forced end quickly. Evaluating all paths, N3 leads to an optimal end, and no further play is possible after terminal nodes. Thus, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N3 or N0. If the player moves to N0, the opponent faces N0 and can move to N5 or N6. From N5, the opponent moves to N2 (terminal), ending the game on the next turn. From N6, the opponent moves to either N9 or N8, both leading to paths eventually ending at terminals. If the first player chooses N3, the opponent can go to N1 (terminal) or N4. N4 leads to terminal, so N1 or N4 end the game immediately. From N3, the opponent has two options, but either leads to a terminal or recursive paths. The best move from N7 is to N3 because it gives immediate control and leads to terminal or a forced end quickly. Evaluating all paths, N3 leads to an optimal end, and no further play is possible after terminal nodes. Thus, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N0\nN3 -> N1, N4\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN6 -> N9, N8\n\nLegal moves: N1, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N1 or N4. N1 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. N4 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Either move results in the current player winning. Since both are terminal moves, either is optimal. Choosing N1 is valid and ends the game. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N1 or N4. N1 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. N4 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Either move results in the current player winning. Since both are terminal moves, either is optimal. Choosing N1 is valid and ends the game. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N11, N9\nN10 -> N2, N3\nN6 -> N5, N13\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N12\nN13 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N13\nN0 -> N1, N7\nN9 -> N11, N10\nN4 -> N0, N6\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the player can move to N0 or N6. Evaluating both:\n\n- Move to N0: leads to N1 or N7.\n   - N1 → N8 or N12 (both lead to terminal or branches to N9, N11 → terminal).\n   - N7 → N3 or N13 (both terminal).\n   - Paths eventually end due to no further moves after reaching terminals.\n   - The game ends when a player cannot move. Since N0 and N7 lead into chains ending at terminal nodes, all paths eventually end with a player unable to move. Backtracking shows this leads to a losing position for the next player (i.e., terminal in a chain of moves).\n\n- Move to N6: leads to N5 or N13.\n   - Both N5 and N13 are terminal (no further moves). So, the opponent is left with no options after your move, meaning you win immediately.\n\nThus, choosing N6 results in immediate end of game with no legal move for the opponent.\n\nTherefore, the optimal choice is N6, as it leads to a winning outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the player can move to N0 or N6. Evaluating both:\n\n- Move to N0: leads to N1 or N7.\n   - N1 → N8 or N12 (both lead to terminal or branches to N9, N11 → terminal).\n   - N7 → N3 or N13 (both terminal).\n   - Paths eventually end due to no further moves after reaching terminals.\n   - The game ends when a player cannot move. Since N0 and N7 lead into chains ending at terminal nodes, all paths eventually end with a player unable to move. Backtracking shows this leads to a losing position for the next player (i.e., terminal in a chain of moves).\n\n- Move to N6: leads to N5 or N13.\n   - Both N5 and N13 are terminal (no further moves). So, the opponent is left with no options after your move, meaning you win immediately.\n\nThus, choosing N6 results in immediate end of game with no legal move for the opponent.\n\nTherefore, the optimal choice is N6, as it leads to a winning outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N11, N9\nN10 -> N2, N3\nN6 -> N5, N13\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N12\nN13 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N13\nN0 -> N1, N7\nN9 -> N11, N10\nN4 -> N0, N6\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N5 or N13. Both N5 and N13 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever move is chosen ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Therefore, both moves are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and both result in immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N13 is valid and terminal. Hence, the optimal choice is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N5 or N13. Both N5 and N13 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, whichever move is chosen ends the game on the next turn, and the opponent has no move. Therefore, both moves are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and both result in immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N13 is valid and terminal. Hence, the optimal choice is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N8, N4\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> N13, N9\nN13 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7\nN8 -> N12, N1, N10\nN5 -> N3, N2, N12\nN2 -> N11, N3, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N3, N2, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N2, or N12. Evaluating each:\n\n- Move to N3: leads to edges to N8 or N4. \n  - N8 → N12, N1, N10; N12 → N6 → terminal; N4 → N7 → terminal. Branches can lead to long chains or terminal states. \n  - From N8 or N4, paths eventually reach terminal or recursive game states. Critical to note that N8 → N12 → N6 → terminal (win for next player), so some paths end quickly with loss.\n- Move to N2: leads to N11, N3, N1. \n  - N11 → N13 (terminal), N9 → N0 (terminal). N3 → branches to N8/N4 → terminal or deeper paths. Again, many end quickly.\n- Move to N12: directly leads to terminal via N6 → terminal. This move ends the game immediately. Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to N12 ends the game immediately and thus wins the game.\n\nThus, choosing N12 immediately ends the game and wins.\n\nTherefore, the optimal move is to N12.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N2, or N12. Evaluating each:\n\n- Move to N3: leads to edges to N8 or N4. \n  - N8 → N12, N1, N10; N12 → N6 → terminal; N4 → N7 → terminal. Branches can lead to long chains or terminal states. \n  - From N8 or N4, paths eventually reach terminal or recursive game states. Critical to note that N8 → N12 → N6 → terminal (win for next player), so some paths end quickly with loss.\n- Move to N2: leads to N11, N3, N1. \n  - N11 → N13 (terminal), N9 → N0 (terminal). N3 → branches to N8/N4 → terminal or deeper paths. Again, many end quickly.\n- Move to N12: directly leads to terminal via N6 → terminal. This move ends the game immediately. Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to N12 ends the game immediately and thus wins the game.\n\nThus, choosing N12 immediately ends the game and wins.\n\nTherefore, the optimal move is to N12.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N8, N4\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> N13, N9\nN13 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7\nN8 -> N12, N1, N10\nN5 -> N3, N2, N12\nN2 -> N11, N3, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N12, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N6 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move is available from N12. Therefore, the optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N12, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N6 ends the game on the next player's turn, making the current player win. No other move is available from N12. Therefore, the optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> N8, N4\nN13 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N11\nN6 -> N9, N12\nN3 -> N6, N13\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N2\nN4 -> N0, N11\nN8 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N8 or N4. Evaluating the outcomes:  \n- Move to N8: from N8, can go to N3 or N7.  \n   - N3 → N6 or N13; both lead to chains that eventually reach terminal or loops.  \n   - N7 → N1 (terminal) or N2 (terminal). Terminal wins for next player, so this is losing.  \n   - N6 → N9 (terminal) or N12 → N2 or N5 (both terminal). All paths end in terminal.  \n   So, from N8, all options end in terminal after one or two moves, meaning the player facing terminal after their turn loses. Thus, if forced, N8 leads to a losing path.  \n- Move to N4: from N4 → N0 or N11.  \n   - N0 → N1 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). Both terminal.  \n   - N11 → terminal.  \n   So, from N4, the next player faces terminal, which is a win.  \nThus, choosing N4 leads to the opponent having no move, so it is a winning strategy. Hence, choosing N4 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N8 or N4. Evaluating the outcomes:  \n- Move to N8: from N8, can go to N3 or N7.  \n   - N3 → N6 or N13; both lead to chains that eventually reach terminal or loops.  \n   - N7 → N1 (terminal) or N2 (terminal). Terminal wins for next player, so this is losing.  \n   - N6 → N9 (terminal) or N12 → N2 or N5 (both terminal). All paths end in terminal.  \n   So, from N8, all options end in terminal after one or two moves, meaning the player facing terminal after their turn loses. Thus, if forced, N8 leads to a losing path.  \n- Move to N4: from N4 → N0 or N11.  \n   - N0 → N1 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). Both terminal.  \n   - N11 → terminal.  \n   So, from N4, the next player faces terminal, which is a win.  \nThus, choosing N4 leads to the opponent having no move, so it is a winning strategy. Hence, choosing N4 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> N8, N4\nN13 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N11\nN6 -> N9, N12\nN3 -> N6, N13\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N2\nN4 -> N0, N11\nN8 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N0, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N0 or N11. N11 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N11 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N11 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N0 or N11. N11 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N11 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N11 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}]
